rt preparing for the mitigation phase  mitigation phase is equivalent to wide  community spread that can easily over  stretch the health services like what we  are seeing now in the European countries  now biologists are also predicting that  African countries will experience their  peak number of infections this summer  now how prepared would you say the  continent is you spoke of upscaling the  capacities Michelle I would say that the  first step culture is perform quite well  by detecting the sporadic cases that  were imported in Africa but for the next  step is where we need to mobilize more  and we need more effort we need also  more support in most of the African  countries because it is a step that they  will have to deal with hundreds of  people needing intensive health care  while in the actual system in many  African and facilities you don't have  such capacity so they need to think  about how to scale up with for temporary  treatment structures how to get access  to oxygen provider and also respiratory  machine how to get access to protective  equipment so at this level countries are  less prepared because they are lacking  these resources most of them have  develop a preparedness and response plan  but the supply chain for example is one  of the major challenge to fulfill this  part that is actually urgent because we  are moving toward the white  contamination in many countries already  now Michelle spoke there of wide  contamination do you all see the crisis  in Africa reaching a similar level to  what we're seeing here and  how would that look like I think I think  that you know that's that's what we've  you know I've been thinking about  exactly that since way back you know  when we were hearing about the outbreak  first of all in wuhan in China I think  one positive is I think that much work  has gone on to try and expand for  example diagnostic testing services  across Africa and we've learnt a great  deal from programs to tackle big  infection hazards such as HIV and  malaria so for example simple rapid  testing technology is now relatively  quick and appropriate to drawl out so we  have these sorts of tests here which can  be done without a laboratory they're not  perfect but they're very easy to scale  out what I'm hoping is that what we've  learnt from those kinds of activities  those kinds of Public Health activities  will help to deal with early  identification and early containment in  these states but it is a huge problem  given that we can't contain outbreaks  very effectively in Europe I think it'll  be the same in any other country across  the world now testing of course a  important part of the response to the  corona virus Michelle would you say that  people are taking the gravity of the  situation  there in Africa seriously I think most  of the national authorities are taking  it seriously if we look at all the state  of emergency declaration that we will  start to record in many African  countries they are taking in seriously  the next step is really for the whole  population and I'm not sure that they  have this level of awareness as well as  I'll take taking it seriously in most of  the African communities there is a lot  of rumors going around including means  for for treatment so there is a work to  be done and from what we learned from  Ebola is that communities need to be  fully on board and for  they need to have appropriate  information to increase awareness but  they need to be fully engaged for them  to play a role if we have a  community-wide transmission is the  community behavior that will impact on  the way the disease will spread faster  within this community and I think  learning from Europe is time now to take  some of the measures public health  measures with full engagement of the  community to at least slow down the  spread and the objective is to flatten  the an eventual peak that will actually  a law may control with the existing  capacities should African countries go  into lockdown we've been asked this a  lot one of the problems that was trying  to give a definitive answer to the  question of whether African countries  should be locking down is that the  situation is just so unprecedented we're  having to interpret data as it  progresses we'll only really know what  measures work best after the pandemic is  over and we can compare what different  countries did and when they did it and  how that affected infection rates and  how it affected mortality what we do  know is that even though model shows  stringent social distancing measures can  help slow the speed of the spread of  this disease  they won't stop it on their own  containment measures like lock downs  also have a huge economic and social  price tag so there's no real clear  advise on one country should Institute  them but I'm pretty certain we're going  to see more of them in the coming days  in Africa as well could saw scope to  mutate quickly and reinfect people who  already had it like old viruses source  code - evolves over time collecting  mutations from generation to generation  we haven't been studying it for very  long but what we've seen so far  indicates that it's evolving more slowly  than for example flu viruses do and and  that's a positive thing flu viruses  mutate so quickly that we have to come  up with new vaccines for them every year  the question of reinfection after you've  already had cope at 19 months this kind  of still up in the air if SARS Cove 2 is  similar  to coronaviruses from the same family  that we also catch like like the viruses  that cause colds then we will develop at  least some immunity but we don't know  yet if it'll be permanent or how long it  might last can mosquitoes spread the  virus no the virus is pretty clearly not  transmitted by a blood transmission was  one of the first things that scientists  looked into after the outbreak began  they examined a range of possible ways  that it could be given to others whether  active virus can be found in blood or a  urine or stool or saliva what they  discovered is that although the virus  can be detected in the blood there's no  evidence at all that it could be  transmitted by a mosquito bite so you  don't need to worry about catching it  that way does drinking liquids regularly  prevent me from catching the virus this  is one of those really insidious myths  because it sounds pretty logical right  in fact the first person who told me  about it and sent me it's something when  this was making its rounds on the  internet a couple of weeks ago was a  doctor unfortunately it's not true  although it sounds really like a great  idea washing all those nasty viruses  down into your stomach where they can be  destroyed by the acid it unfortunately  doesn't work that way sipping water  every couple of minutes is not going to  prevent you from getting Co vat19 the  only health benefit that sipping that  water is going to have is to help you  keep hydrated now around the world  celebrities have inundated us with their  song and dance numbers often from home  and with mixed reception but Ugandan  musician turned politician Bobby wine  has seen his own take widely shared it's  a music video reminding everyone about  how to curb the spread of the corona  virus wash your hands and keep your  distance have a listen  [Music]  [Music]  Bobby wine there but we're now joined by  our correspondent joy Doreen Bharat  she's standing by for us in Nairobi  Kenya  joy we just listen to Bobby wines wrap /  public service message there for us what  sort of role do pop culture and social  media have in getting the word out a lot  actually because when we look at the  African demographics at the moment a big  population happen to fall between the  ages of 35 going down and this is where  the major concern is for a lot of people  who right now are focusing on making  sure that the young people get the  message because the older generation at  the moment does not have access to  technology on a large scale and they are  or happen to be the most vulnerable  demographic when it comes to the spread  of the corona virus or kovat 19 so  social media and pop culture is playing  a humungous role in spreading the  message to most of the young people on  the African continent and in Kenya and  Uganda as well particularly so young  people acting as multipliers of  the message there now that message  itself it seems quite simple wash your  hands stay home but neither of those  things are easy for everyone indeed  access to water is not readily available  to millions of people around the African  continent and  that in itself is a challenge especially  in the informal settlements in a lot of  areas the rural parts of Africa per se  in Kenya today what the government is  trying to do is ask the water suppliers  to be a little bit lenient with their  customers not push up their bills and  also do not switch off their water  connections in a bid to help people  flatten the curve of the corona virus  spread that water will be readily  available to the people and that even  though they might be having a race  they're still going to be able to wash  their hands and maybe take care of that  problem at a later date and also the  government has tried as much as possible  to go to areas where there is not enough  access to water to sensitize them and  also provide water where necessary thank  you very much our correspondent a joy  Doreen birra there for us in Nairobi

